The tree listened with patient rings, each one a year of listening to the world tremble and heal. When the traveler spoke of fear, the tree exhaled a breeze that carried the scent of rain on dry earth. When he spoke of hunger, the branches offered fruit that tasted like home, even when it was far away. The traveler learned to ask instead of demand, to listen as the wind listened to the branches.

One night, the traveler found a small bird perched on a branch. The bird wore a thread of starlight around its neck and spoke in a language the traveler gradually understood—a language of turning, of becoming, of letting go. The compass finally spun to a stop, but not toward a city or a shore. It pointed inward, guiding him to the place where courage grows like moss on old stone.

He stayed, teaching others to listen. The tree kept shining, not to point the way, but to remind everyone that directions are promises we give to ourselves.
